{"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Explain why the Moon has phases in six short paragraphs of plain text."},{"role":"assistant","content":"The Moon doesn't make its own light. It shines because sunlight reflects off its surface. At any moment, the Sun lights up half of the Moon, the same way it lights up half of Earth. Phases come from how much of that lit half we can see from Earth.\n\nThe Moon orbits Earth about once every 27.3 days. As it travels, the angle between the Sun, Earth, and Moon keeps changing. The Sun always lights the side of the Moon that faces it, but our view of that lit side changes from night to night.\n\nAt new moon, the Moon sits roughly between Earth and the Sun. The lit half faces away from us, and the side facing Earth is dark. That is why the Moon is nearly invisible then.\n\nAfter new moon, the Moon moves along its orbit and we begin to see a thin sliver of the lit side. This is the waxing crescent. About a week after new moon, we see half of the lit side, which looks like a half-lit disk. This is first quarter. The name refers to the Moon being one quarter of the way around its orbit, not to how much of it we see.\n\nAt full moon, about two weeks after new moon, Earth lies roughly between the Sun and the Moon. We see the entire lit side, so the Moon looks like a bright, full circle. After that the lit part appears to shrink. This is the waning phase, and it passes through last quarter and then a thin waning crescent.\n\nThe whole cycle takes about 29.5 days, a little longer than the orbit itself. That's because Earth also moves around the Sun, so the Moon has to travel a bit farther to line up with the Sun again. A common myth says phases are caused by Earth's shadow. That shadow only causes lunar eclipses, which are much rarer."}],"title":"Why the Moon Has Phases","tokens_used":428}
