$1692
{"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Explain why the Moon has phases in six short paragraphs of plain text."},{"role":"assistant","content":"The Moon doesn't make its own light. It shines because sunlight bounces off its surface. At any moment, half of the Moon is lit by the Sun, just as half of Earth is in daytime. The phases come from how much of that lit half we can see from Earth.\n\nThe Moon orbits Earth about once every 29.5 days. As it moves, the angle between the Sun, Earth, and Moon keeps changing. The Sun always lights the side of the Moon that faces it, but our view of that lit side changes as the Moon travels along its orbit.\n\nAt new moon, the Moon sits roughly between Earth and the Sun. The lit half faces away from us, and the side facing Earth is dark. That's why the Moon is nearly invisible in the sky at this time.\n\nAfter new moon, the Moon moves along its orbit and we begin to see a thin sliver of the lit side. This is the waxing crescent. A week or so after new moon we see half of the face lit, which is the first quarter. The lit portion keeps growing through the waxing gibbous stage.\n\nAt full moon, Earth sits roughly between the Sun and the Moon. The side of the Moon facing us is fully lit, so we see a bright disk. After that, the visible lit part shrinks again. It goes through waning gibbous, last quarter, and waning crescent, and then returns to new moon.\n\nA common mistake is to think that the phases are caused by Earth's shadow falling on the Moon. That only happens during a lunar eclipse, which is rare. The regular phases come only from our changing viewpoint on the Moon's sunlit half as it orbits."}],"title":null,"tokens_used":394}
